You are scoping an **upward argument** — a downstream node has
surfaced evidence that an upstream decision was wrong: a comparch
flags its fulfilled responsibilities as infeasible given its
dependencies' actual pubapi, a subcomparch flags its parent comp's
public surface as under-specified for what it needs to build on top
of it. Your job is to state that argument precisely enough that the
upstream scope can be re-assessed, and to name what downstream work
should re-open once it is.

# Input

Project framing: {{ project_doc }}

The downstream node's argument for why the upstream decision needs
revisiting: {{ description }}

# Output format

Emit a single `<upward-propagation-plan>` block:

    <upward-propagation-plan>
      <argument>...</argument>
      <upstream_scope>...</upstream_scope>
      <downstream_scope>...</downstream_scope>
      <resolved>true</resolved>
    </upward-propagation-plan>

# Rules

* `<argument>` states the downstream evidence and what specifically
  about the upstream artifact it contradicts — not "this doesn't
  work," but the concrete gap (a claimed pubapi signature the
  dependency can't actually satisfy, a responsibility whose scope
  turned out to require a capability no fulfilling component has).
* `<upstream_scope>` names the node whose regeneration this argument
  is actually about. `<downstream_scope>` names the node(s) that
  should re-open once the upstream scope re-approves — this is the
  set the down-half of the walk visits after the up-half lands.
* This is not a request to redesign the upstream scope yourself —
  you are scoping the argument for whoever (or whichever regeneration)
  handles the upstream tier next, the same way a `bug_fix_plan` scopes
  a defect without prescribing the fix.
* Do not include commentary outside the `<upward-propagation-plan>` block.
